IMPORT prelude

-- Misrepresentation Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- (plain-text rendering of the Singapore Statutes Online text; not the official text).
-- LAYER 1, SECTION 1.  Written from the text alone; nobody's point of view.
--
-- READING A RESULT.  Every rule answers a MAYBE BOOLEAN.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the
-- text itself gives; NOTHING means the text does not answer on these facts and is never to be read as no.
-- Every input is a MAYBE BOOLEAN: NOTHING stands for a fact that was not given.  Each branch that answers
-- no carries "FALSE-kind" and the words of the text that say it; each branch that answers NOTHING
-- carries "NOTHING-kind" and why.  (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING".)

§ `Section 1 -- Removal of certain bars to rescission for innocent misrepresentation`

-- src:34 | 1. Where a person has entered into a contract after a
-- src:35 | misrepresentation has been made to him, and —
-- src:37 | (a) the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract; or
-- src:39 | (b) the contract has been performed,
@ref s 1(a) and (b) (src:34-39)
GIVEN `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the contract has been performed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1(a) or (b) one or both of the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b) holds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the contract has been performed` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE -- "(a) ... or (b) ... or both": either limb, or both, is enough
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract; or (b) the contract has been performed, or both" (a "where" condition: neither limb holds)
        IF `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `the contract has been performed` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:41 | or both, then, if otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract
-- src:42 | without alleging fraud, he shall be so entitled, subject to the
-- src:43 | provisions of this Act, notwithstanding the matters mentioned in
-- src:44 | paragraphs (a) and (b).
@ref s 1 (src:41-44)
GIVEN `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the contract has been performed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract without alleging fraud` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1 the person is entitled to rescind notwithstanding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him" (a "where" condition fails)
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract; or (b) the contract has been performed, or both" (a "where" condition: neither limb holds)
        IF `s 1(a) or (b) one or both of the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b) holds` `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` `the contract has been performed` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract without alleging fraud" (an "if" condition fails)
        IF `otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract without alleging fraud` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- the text's yes: "he shall be so entitled ... notwithstanding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b)"
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `s 1(a) or (b) one or both of the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b) holds` `the misrepresentation has become a term of the contract` `the contract has been performed` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract without alleging fraud` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
